[%# /var/tmp, which is where ubuntu.setup.sh untars the payload.  These two said
    /tmp and so found nothing, every run, on every guest -- and the "; /bin/true"
    after them meant the error was printed and then discarded, so lexicon went
    unpatched and only failed later, deep in a dehydrated hook, with a
    MissingSchema on the socket path it cannot use unpatched.

    Applied only when not already in: one of these went upstream, so a lexicon
    that already has it is the expected case rather than a fault, and "git apply"
    of an applied patch fails.  A patch that neither applies nor reverses says so
    without stopping the build -- the guest test asks lexicon whether it can
    reach the socket, which is the thing actually needed. -%]
[% FOR patch IN ['lexicon-pdns-af-unix', 'lexicon-arbitrary-record-types'] -%]
cd / && { git apply --reverse --check /var/tmp/domainsetup_[% domain %]/[% patch %].patch >/dev/null 2>&1 && echo "[% patch %]: already applied"; } || git apply /var/tmp/domainsetup_[% domain %]/[% patch %].patch || echo "[% patch %]: WOULD NOT APPLY -- lexicon may be missing it; see templates/files/patches" >&2
[% END -%]
